# Shell scripts are executed by bash (Git Bash, WSL), where a CRLF ending
# turns `set -euo pipefail` into `set: pipefail\r: invalid option name`.
# Pinned to LF so core.autocrlf=true checkouts on Windows cannot break them.
*.sh text eol=lf

# The two files vendored BYTE FOR BYTE out of the crucible repo. Both are
# written LF by `scripts/gen-modules.py`, and the doctrine for the module is
# that posting it means posting exactly those bytes — a hand edit, or a line
# ending this repo added on the way out, is a different file.
#
# core.autocrlf=true is set on the machine this was found on, and a fresh
# checkout there wrote foundry.module.json as 467 CRLF bytes where canonical is
# 435 LF. Nothing catches that: the JSON still parses, the working copy anyone
# looks at is whatever their git wrote, and the difference only shows up in
# something that hashes the file rather than the parsed value. Same reason the
# shell scripts above are pinned, one file type along.
app/shared/foundry.module.json text eol=lf
app/shared/model-lineup.json text eol=lf
